status: complete
goal: Separate live and historical data paths. Eliminate two-writer conflict. Implement kdb+-inspired RDB/HDB pattern adapted for 24/7 crypto with hourly bar granularity.
started: 2026-03-14
type: infrastructure
trigger: |
  Quant data review found two-writer conflict: build_parquet_cache.py and live_fetcher.py both
  write to data/{market}/1h_cache/*.parquet with no coordination. Race condition causes data loss.
  Research into kdb+ (KX Systems), ArcticDB (Man Group), and quant best practices all converge on
  the same solution: separate live and historical data stores, merge at read time.
baseline:
  current_architecture: |
    CSV (fetch scripts) → build_parquet_cache.py → data/{market}/1h_cache/*.parquet ← live_fetcher.py also writes
    Two writers, no coordination, no reconciliation, no versioning.
  bugs_found: |
    - Mixed index types (integer vs DatetimeIndex) when live_fetcher appends to historical parquet
    - Binance funding timestamp jitter (3-4ms offset) causes missed funding merges
    - 101-hour data gap (Mar 5-10) from fetch scripts not running
    - 3 problem tokens (LIT, XMR, PAXG) with spot/perp data incompatibility
  references: |
    - kdb+ tick architecture: TP → RDB (live, in-memory) → HDB (historical, on-disk)
    - ArcticDB (Man Group): versioned, append-only, bi-temporal time series
    - Two Sigma: data-as-a-service with immutable lake + live overlay
    - Citadel Securities: cloud-native hot/cold tiers with reconciliation gates

